          '**McGowan Classification of Ulnar Neuropathy Severity**:\n- **Grade I (Mild)**: Intermittent paresthesias, no weakness or atrophy, subjective complaints only\n- **Grade II (Moderate)**: Constant paresthesias, measurable weakness, early muscle wasting. **This patient is McGowan Grade II** (weakness of FDI 3+/5, positive Froment, visible atrophy).\n- **Grade III (Severe)**: Marked intrinsic atrophy, severe weakness, claw deformity\n\n**Dellon Modification**:\n- Adds subclassification of Grade II into IIA (fair muscle strength) and IIB (poor muscle strength)\n\n**EMG Severity Assessment**:\n- **Mild/demyelinating**: Conduction slowing or block across elbow, normal SNAP amplitude, no denervation on EMG\n- **Moderate**: Conduction slowing PLUS reduced CMAP amplitude, reduced SNAP, fibrillations in intrinsics\n- **Severe**: Absent or very low CMAP, absent SNAP, dense denervation in multiple ulnar muscles, reduced recruitment\n\n**Treatment Decision Based on Severity**:\n- **Mild (McGowan I)**: Conservative management first (activity modification, splinting) for 3-6 months\n- **Moderate (McGowan II) with axonal loss on EDX**: This is a clinical inflection point. If symptoms are progressive and EDX shows active denervation, **surgical consultation is warranted** because axonal loss indicates ongoing nerve damage that may not reverse with conservative measures alone.\n- **Severe (McGowan III)**: Surgical decompression indicated, though outcomes are less favorable once severe atrophy has developed.\n\n**Prognostic Factors for Recovery**:\n- Shorter duration of symptoms → better outcomes\n- Demyelinating (conduction block) → better prognosis than axonal loss\n- Younger age → better nerve regeneration potential\n- McGowan Grade I-II → significantly better surgical outcomes than Grade III',
