ssion**: Release of the cubital tunnel retinaculum without moving the nerve. Simplest procedure, good outcomes for mild-moderate cases.\n2. **Medial epicondylectomy**: Removal of the medial epicondyle to decompress the nerve.\n3. **Anterior transposition** (subcutaneous, intramuscular, or submuscular): Moves the nerve anterior to the medial epicondyle. Used when nerve subluxes or when in-situ decompression fails. Submuscular transposition may have best outcomes for severe cases.\n\n**Post-surgical rehabilitation**: Immobilization for 1-3 weeks depending on procedure, then progressive ROM and strengthening. Nerve recovery follows Seddon/Sunderland classification — expect 1 mm/day (1 inch/month) for axonal regeneration. Full recovery may take 6-12 months.',
