nd I will support whatever you choose. But I would not be doing my job if I did not tell you that the window for the best surgical outcomes is narrowing as the nerve damage progresses. Here is what I would suggest: meet with the surgeon for a consultation. Ask every question you have. You are not committing to surgery by having a conversation. After that, you can make a fully informed decision.\'\n\n**6. Offer a compromise and timeline**:\n\'If you are truly not ready for surgery, I understand. What I would ask is that we repeat the nerve test in 6-8 weeks. If the nerve is stable, we have some time. If it is getting worse, I would strongly encourage you to reconsider. Can we agree on that plan?\'\n\n**Key principles**: Informed consent AND informed refusal, honest prognosis presentation, respecting patient autonomy, addressing specific fears rather than generic reassurance, offering incremental steps (consultation vs. commitment), establishing a monitoring plan as a safety net.',
